":"Anna Harrison"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" She was the last First Lady to have been born in British America.","title":"Anna Harrison"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Edith Kermit Carow Roosevelt (August 6, 1861 – September 30, 1948) was the second wife of President Theodore Roosevelt and served as First Lady of the United States during his presidency from 1901 to 1909.","title":"Edith Roosevelt"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" She was the first First Lady to employ a full-time, salaried social secretary.","title":"Edith Roosevelt"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Her tenure resulted in the creation of an official staff, and her formal dinners and ceremonial processions served to elevate the position of First Lady.","title":"Edith Roosevelt"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Vicki Risch is the former First Lady of Idaho and the wife of U.S. Sen. Jim Risch, who served as Governor of Idaho in 2006.","title":"Vicki Risch"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" She became First Lady on May 26, 2006, when her husband succeeded former Gov. Dirk Kempthorne, who resigned to become United States Secretary of the Interior.","title":"Vicki Risch"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Mrs. Risch succeeded former First Lady Patricia Kempthorne who had held the post for over seven years.","title":"Vicki Risch"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Mrs. Risch served as First Lady until January 2007, as her husband did not seek a full term as governor, but rather was reelected to his old post as lieutenant governor.","title":"Vicki Risch"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The First Lady of the United States is the hostess of the White House.","title":"List of First Ladies of the United States"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The position is traditionally filled by the wife of the President of the United States, but, on occasion, the title has been applied to women who were not presidents’ wives, such as when the president was a bachelor or widower, or when the wife of the president was unable to fulfill the duties of the First Lady herself.","title":"