{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Hans Wilhelm Gatzke (1915–1987) was a historian of German foreign policy since World War I.","title":"Hans W. Gatzke"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Born in Dülken, Germany, he attended the University of Bonn and the University of Munich, finally graduating from Williams College in 1938.","title":"Hans W. Gatzke"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" After serving in the United States Army during World War II as a second lieutenant, he received his M.A. (1939) and PhD.","title":"Hans W. Gatzke"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" (1947) from Harvard University.","title":"Hans W. Gatzke"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" He taught at Johns Hopkins University from 1947 to 1964, during which time he was awarded a Guggenheim Fellowship in 1956.","title":"Hans W. Gatzke"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" He joined the department of history at Yale University in 1965 and remained there until his retirement in 1986.","title":"Hans W. Gatzke"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Gatzke anonymously funded a prize, awarded biennially by the American Historical Association, in honor of Paul M. Birdsall (who was Dean of Students at Williams when Gatzke arrived there in the late 1930s) for the best work in the field of European military or strategic history since 1870.","title":"Hans W. Gatzke"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" His involvement was revealed upon his death in 1987.","title":"Hans W. Gatzke"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" He is remembered by a named professorship in his honor (the Hans W. Gatzke '38 Professor of Modern European History) at Williams College.","title":"Hans W. Gatzke"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Zephaniah Swift Moore (November 20, 1770 – June 29, 1823) was an American Congregational clergyman and educator.","title":"Zephaniah Swift Moore"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He taught at Dartmouth College during the early 1810s and had a house built in Hanover, New Hampshire that now serves as Dartmouth's Blunt Alumni Center.","title":"Zephaniah Swift Moore"},{"sentence_id":2,"text"