, Illinois, from 1912 until his retirement in 1947.","title":"Edward E. Wilson"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Wilson died in France while on vacation on February 21, 1952.","title":"Edward E. Wilson"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Captain Byron Curtis Weston (April 9, 1832 – November 8, 1898) was a native of Massachusetts who founded the Weston Paper Company in 1863 (which ceased to exist following its sale in 2008) and served as the 32nd Lieutenant Governor of Massachusetts from 1880 to 1883.","title":"Byron Weston"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He came from an old New England Congregationalist family of extraordinary wealth.","title":"Byron Weston"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In 1865, he married Julia Clark Mitchell, with whom he had ten children, including Julia Carolyn Weston, mother to the well known chef Julia Child.","title":"Byron Weston"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" They lived in a mansion known as Westonholme, in Dalton, Massachusetts.","title":"Byron Weston"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Weston was known for his gifts to the community, including the Grace Episcopal Church in his hometown and funds towards the debt incurred for the grading and draining of an athletic field and monies toward upkeep and a grandstand at Williams College.","title":"Byron Weston"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Weston received an honorary M.A. from Williams College in 1886 and the field, still used today, was named Weston Field in his honor.","title":"Byron Weston"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Raymond Chang (1939-2017) was an emeritus professor at Williams College in the Department of Chemistry and a textbook author.","title":"Raymond Chang (chemist)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" His most popular textbook was titled "Chemistry," which was published up to the twelfth edition.","title":"Raymond Chang (chemist)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He also published a few children's books.","title":"Raymond Chang (chemist)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He received his B.Sc.","title":"Raymond Chang (chemist)"},{"sentenc