{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Bearhead Mountain (8406 ft ) is located in the Lewis Range, Glacier National Park in the U.S. state of Montana.","title":"Bearhead Mountain (Montana)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The mountain is named for "kyáiyótokan", a Piegan warrior known for his war against the dreaded Assiniboine "White Dog".","title":"Bearhead Mountain (Montana)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" "kyáiyótokan" was also a survivor of the Marias Massacre and the brother of "Chief Heavy Runner" a confidant of Glacier author James Willard Schultz.","title":"Bearhead Mountain (Montana)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Joseph Horn Cloud born 1873 and died September 18, 1920.","title":"Joseph Horn Cloud"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" His father was Horn Cloud and mother Nest, both parents died at the Wounded Knee Massacre along with two brothers William Horn Cloud (1876-1890), Sherman Horn Cloud (1865-1890) and a niece.","title":"Joseph Horn Cloud"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Joseph was a survivor of the massacre as well as his brother Daniel and half brother Dewey Beard [p. 89-95].","title":"Joseph Horn Cloud"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Dewey's mother was Yellow Leaf who also died during the massacre.","title":"Joseph Horn Cloud"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In the early 1900s a writer named Eli S. Ricker began research for a book he was going to call "The Final Conflict between the Red Men and the Palefaces."","title":"Joseph Horn Cloud"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" He gathered sources and interviews about conditions and battles on the Plains during the last half of the 1800s.","title":"Joseph Horn Cloud"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" He recorded the interviews in small note pads known to historians as the "Ricker Tablets."","title":"Joseph Horn Cloud"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" One such interview was with Joseph telling Ricker what he witnessed at the Wounded Knee Massacre, "When the shooting began the women ran to the ravine.","title":"Joseph Horn Cloud"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" The shooting was in every direction.","title":"Joseph Horn Cloud"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" Soldiers shot into one another... Many of the Indians in the circle were killed.","title":"Joseph Horn Cloud"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" Many of them mingled with the soldiers behind them, picking up guns from dead soldiers and taking cartridge belts."","title":"Joseph Horn Cloud"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Westervelt massacre, also known as the Westerfield massacre, was an indigenous attack on a caravan of Dutch frontier settlers that occurred during the American Revolutionary War around 3:00 am on June 27, 1780 in Kentucky County, Virginia, the present day state of Kentucky.","title":"Westervelt massacre"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It remains one of the largest massacres in Kentucky state history.","title":"Westervelt massacre"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The settlers were traveling southeast from Low Dutch Station to Harrod's Town.","title":"Westervelt massacre"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The settler relocation was in part a reaction to British Captain Henry Bird's invasion of Kentucky.","title":"Westervelt massacre"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The area immediately east of Low Dutch Station had been overrun with British allied Indians.","title":"Westervelt massacre"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Harrod's Town was fortified and a move south would lead the settlers away from Captain Bird's invading army from the north.","title":"Westervelt massacre"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The caravan was ambushed in a surprise attack, during the night, after a day's travel of twelve miles.","title":"Westervelt massacre"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" The exact location of the massacre is not definitively known.","title":"Westervelt massacre"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" However, it is likely to have occurred at Floyd's Fork and Broad Run.","title":"Westervelt massacre"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" The caravan was formed by Jacobus Westervelt and consisted of forty-one settlers from ten different families.","title":"Westervelt massacre"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" Ten of the seventeen settlers killed were members of the Westervelt family.","title":"Westervelt massacre"},{"sentence_id":11,"text":" The victims included men, women, and children.","title":"Westervelt massacre"},{"sentence_id":12,"text":" The Indians responsible for the massacre were allied to the British and received ₤5 for each victim's scalp cut off and returned to the British authorities.","title":"Westervelt massacre"},{"sentence_id":13,"text":" The Indians were thus awarded ₤85 by the British for massacring the Dutch settlers.","title":"Westervelt massacre"},{"sentence_id":14,"text":" The Westervelt Massacre had a chilling effect on the region.","title":"Westervelt massacre"},{"sentence_id":15,"text":" A number of settlers from Low Dutch Station joined Colonel George Rogers Clark's militia after the massacre.","title":"Westervelt massacre"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Penn's Creek massacre was a massacre and Indian raid on October 16, 1755 near Penn's Creek where it flows through Selinsgrove, Pennsylvania, US.","title":"Penn's Creek massacre"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The Indians killed all but one of the 25 settlers; a man who managed to escape and notify local authorities.","title":"Penn's Creek massacre"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The Indians responsible for the raid were Delaware Indians.","title":"Penn's Creek massacre"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" They were dressed in war paint and war costumes during the attack.","title":"Penn's Creek massacre"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The area near where the massacre took place was also later rumored to be haunted.","title":"Penn's Creek massacre"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Indian South Africans are citizens and residents of South Africa that are of Indian descent.","title":"Indian South Africans"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The majority live in and around the city of Durban, making it "the largest 'Indian' city outside India".","title":"Indian South Africans"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Many Indians in South Africa are descendents of migrants from colonial India (South Asia) during late 19th-century through early 20th-century.","title":"Indian South Africans"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" At times Indians were subsumed in the broader geographical category "Asians".","title":"Indian South Africans"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Indian Actors Association was formed around 1936 and was a non-profit Hollywood based organization.","title":"Indian Actors Association"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" They formed after and were influenced by the War Paint Club, an organization meant to protect rights of Native American actors.","title":"Indian Actors Association"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Additionally, driven by the Hollywood practice of casting non-native actors as Indians in films, the Native actors working in Los Angeles at the time seized their opportunity to establish a pool of “authentic” Indian actors to work in film.","title":"Indian Actors Association"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Their goal was also to keep Indian portrayals and culture from being misrepresented or warped.","title":"Indian Actors Association"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The Indian Actors Association fought for equality and employment for Indian actors at a time when they were not seen as important or equal.","title":"Indian Actors Association"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The country’s economic depression and the Western movie hiatus left many Indian actors unemployed, which caused depression and poverty on the reservations resulting in many Indians looking for employment elsewhere.","title":"Indian Actors Association"}],"peer_messages":[" half of the 1800s.","title":"Joseph Horn Cloud"}
