{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Pacific Edge Voices (formerly The Pacific Mozart Ensemble (PME)) is a volunteer choral organization based in Berkeley, CA.","title":"Pacific Mozart Ensemble"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The group was formed to provide a chorus of professional quality for highly skilled and experienced singers who did not wish to make singing a full-time profession.","title":"Pacific Mozart Ensemble"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It was to be large enough to perform the major concert literature, but small enough to remain highly selective.","title":"Pacific Mozart Ensemble"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" PEV presents a wide range of choral musical styles, including, but not limited to, traditional choral literature, new works by contemporary composers and a cappella jazz and pop.","title":"Pacific Mozart Ensemble"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" PEV performs at least three self-produced concerts sets each year, along with various collaborations, often with prominent artists including Dave Brubeck, Meredith Monk, Kent Nagano & Sufjan Stevens.","title":"Pacific Mozart Ensemble"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The first and second concerts of the year (typically Nov and March) are classically oriented programs.","title":"Pacific Mozart Ensemble"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Over the years these programs have tended toward 20th-century composers.","title":"Pacific Mozart Ensemble"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" The chorus has become known around the San Francisco Bay Area for its innovative programming.","title":"Pacific Mozart Ensemble"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" A particular highlight came in 2002 when the chorus performed Kurt Weill’s Der Lindberghflug alongside works by Philip Glass, Meredith Monk and David Lang.","title":"Pacific Mozart Ensemble"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" The concert was presented in the East Bay on the aircraft carrier Hornet and in San Francisco in the newly constructed Aviation Museum at SFO.","title":"Pacific Mozart Ensemble"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" The 3rd concert set each year is an all a cappella ‘pops’ concert featuring the group in various formations from 2 up to 50, performing arrangements of jazz, pop, rock, & folk tunes.","title":"Pacific Mozart Ensemble"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Der Widerspänstigen Zähmung (also: Der Widerspenstigen Zähmung ) (English: "The Taming of the Shrew") is a German-language comic opera in four acts by the German composer Hermann Goetz.","title":"Der Widerspänstigen Zähmung"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was written between 1868 and 1872 and first performed at the National Theatre Mannheim on 11 October 1874 under the conductor Ernst Frank.","title":"Der Widerspänstigen Zähmung"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The libretto, by and the composer, is based on Shakespeare's "The Taming of the Shrew".","title":"Der Widerspänstigen Zähmung"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The style of the opera shows Goetz turning away from the musical ideas of Richard Wagner towards the classicism of Mozart.","title":"Der Widerspänstigen Zähmung"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" "Der Widerspänstigen Zähmung " was a huge success, not only in Germany but in the United States and in Great Britain, where it received high praise from George Bernard Shaw.","title":"Der Widerspänstigen Zähmung"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Violin Sonata No. 33 in E-flat major (K. 481) was composed by Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart in Vienna on December 12, 1785.","title":"Violin Sonata No. 33 (Mozart)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was published on its own by Franz Anton Hoffmeister, a German composer and music publisher to whom Mozart's String Quartet No. 20 (K. 499) is dedicated.","title":"Violin Sonata No. 33 (Mozart)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" While much is unknown about the history of this sonata, it is nevertheless a very mature work, written in what one may call Mozart's period of greatest mastery (during his life in Vienna).","title":"Violin Sonata No. 33 (Mozart)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Flight across the Ocean ("Der Ozeanflug") is a "Lehrstück" by the German dramatist Bertolt Brecht, inspired by "We", Charles Lindbergh's 1927 account of his transatlantic flight.","title":"The Flight Across the Ocean"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Written for the Baden-Baden Music Festival, it was originally entitled "Lindbergh's Flight" ("Der Lindberghflug") and premiered in 1929 with music by Kurt Weill and Paul Hindemith in a broadcast by the Southwest German Radio Orchestra under the direction of Hermann Scherchen.","title":"The Flight Across the Ocean"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Tutor is the 1950 adaptation, by 20th century German dramatist Bertolt Brecht, of an 18th-century play by Lenz.","title":"The Tutor (Brecht)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The original Lenz play was produced in 1774 and is also known by the title "The Advantages of a Private Education".","title":"The Tutor (Brecht)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Brecht contributed few additions to the plot of the original work, but made many cuts and alterations.","title":"The Tutor (Brecht)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Brecht's work is two thirds the length of the original play and over half the material is new.","title":"The Tutor (Brecht)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The play was Brecht's first production which featured work from the German Classical Era for the Berliner Ensemble.","title":"The Tutor (Brecht)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Overall, it was the third production the Berliner Ensemble performed.","title":"The Tutor (Brecht)"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Brecht himself directed this production.","title":"The Tutor (Brecht)"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" 'The Tutor' was translated by Ralph Manheim and Wolfgang Sauerlander.","title":"The Tutor (Brecht)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Zeitoper (German: "opera of the time") was a short-lived genre of opera associated with Weimar Germany.","title":"Zeitoper"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is not known when or by whom the term was coined, but by 1928 Kurt Weill ("Zeitoper" in "Melos") was able to complain that it was more a slogan than a description.","title":"Zeitoper"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Like "opera buffa" it used contemporary settings and characters, comic or at least satiric plots ('s "Maschinist Hopkins" is a sole tragic example) and aimed at musical accessibility.","title":"Zeitoper"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Two distinguishing characteristics are a tendency to incorporate modern technology ("Jonny spielt auf": trains, "Der Lindberghflug": airplanes, "Von Heute auf Morgen": telephones, and even elevators) and frequent allusions to popular music, especially jazz.","title":"Zeitoper"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" This last, more than any social satire, earned the suspicion of the political right and ensured that it would not survive into the Nazi era.","title":"Zeitoper"}],"peer_messages":["Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Pacific Edge Voices (formerly The Pacific Mozart Ensemble (PME)) is a volunteer choral organization based in Berkeley, CA.","title":"Pacific Mozart Ensemble"}
