{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Journaille " is a German pejorative term used to refer to tabloid journalism and the yellow press.","title":"Journaille"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The term is a neologism from the early 20th century, formed from the word journalism and the French loan-word "kanaille" ("canaille"), meaning scum, scoundrel or rabble.","title":"Journaille"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The term was introduced by the Austrian writer Karl Kraus in an article in his journal "Die Fackel" in 1902.","title":"Journaille"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In a later article in the same journal, Kraus wrote that the original inventor of the term was the Austrian dramaturge Alfred von Berger.","title":"Journaille"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The pejorative term was much used by the German Nazi Party in their attacks on the press of the Weimar Republic.","title":"Journaille"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Unlike many other terms used by the Nazis, the word "journaille " is still used in present-day Germany, and has also established itself in the political parlance of the Netherlands and Flanders.","title":"Journaille"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Leopold Ritter von Sacher-Masoch (27 January 1836 – 9 March 1895) was an Austrian writer and journalist, who gained renown for his romantic stories of Galician life.","title":"Leopold von Sacher-Masoch"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The term masochism is derived from his name.","title":"Leopold von Sacher-Masoch"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Nathan Birnbaum (Hebrew: נתן בירנבוים‎ ‎ ; pseudonyms: "Mathias Acher", "Dr. N. Birner", "Mathias Palme", "Anton Skart", "Theodor Schwarz", and "Pantarhei") (16 May 1864 – 2 April 1937) was an Austrian writer and journalist, Jewish thinker and nationalist.","title":"Nathan Birnbaum"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" His life had three main phases, representing a progression in his thinking: a Zionist phase (c. 1883 – c. 1900); a Jewish cultural autonomy phase (c. 1900 – c. 1914) which included the promotion of the Yiddish language; and religious phase (c. 1914–1937) when he turned to Orthodox Judaism and became staunchly anti-Zionist.","title":"Nathan Birnbaum"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Stephan Templ is an Austrian writer and journalist who is best known as the co-author of the book "Unser Wien (Our Vienna)" that details how hundreds of Jewish businesses in Vienna were seized by the Nazis and never given back.","title":"Stephan Templ"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Karl Kraus (April 28, 1874 – June 12, 1936) was an Austrian writer and journalist, known as a satirist, essayist, aphorist, playwright and poet.","title":"Karl Kraus (writer)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He directed his satire at the press, German culture, and German and Austrian politics.","title":"Karl Kraus (writer)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The Austrian author Stefan Zweig once called Kraus "the master of venomous ridicule" ("der Meister des giftigen Spotts").","title":"Karl Kraus (writer)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Short Letter, Long Farewell (German: Der kurze Brief zum langen Abschied ) is a 1972 novel by the Austrian writer Peter Handke.","title":"Short Letter, Long Farewell"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It tells the story of a young Austrian writer who travels across the United States in search of his wife from whom he is estranged.","title":"Short Letter, Long Farewell"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The film-director John Ford appears as a character who brings resolution at the end of the road on the coast of California.","title":"Short Letter, Long Farewell"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" His film Young Mr. Lincoln also serves as a point of reference and an antidote to the alienation experienced by the stranger crossing the States.","title":"Short Letter, Long Farewell"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The novel shares many themes and motifs with the film "Alice in the Cities" from 1974, directed by Handke's frequent collaborator Wim Wenders; the film can be seen as a response to the book.","title":"Short Letter, Long Farewell"}],"peer_messages":[", and has also established itself in the political parlance of the Netherlands and Flanders.","title":"Journaille"}
